Supreme People's Court

Circular on Issuing the "Implementation Measures of the People's Courts for Implementing the Provisions on Protecting Judicial Personnel in Lawfully Performing Their Duties"

Fa Fa [2017] No. 4

Higher People's Courts of all provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government; the Military Court of the People's Liberation Army; the Production and Construction Corps Branch of the Higher People's Court of Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region:

The "Implementation Measures of the People's Courts for Implementing the Provisions on Protecting Judicial Personnel in Lawfully Performing Their Duties" are hereby issued to you for your earnest implementation in light of actual circumstances. Any issues or suggestions arising during implementation shall be promptly reported to the Supreme People's Court.

February 7, 2017

Implementation Measures of the People's Courts for Implementing the Provisions on Protecting Judicial Personnel in Lawfully Performing Their Duties

In order to implement the "Provisions on Protecting Judicial Personnel in Lawfully Performing Their Duties" issued by the General Office of the Communist Party of China Central Committee and the General Office of the State Council, to improve and perfect the protection mechanism for judges and trial support personnel in lawfully performing their statutory duties, and to ensure that people's courts independently and impartially exercise their adjudicative power in accordance with the law, these Measures are formulated in light of the actual work of the courts.

Article 1: When a judge handles a case according to law, it shall not be subject to interference by administrative organs, social organizations, or individuals. The judge has the right to refuse to comply with any requirements from any unit or individual that violate statutory duties or statutory procedures or impede judicial justice. For any letter, document, or oral opinion concerning a specific case submitted by any unit or individual outside the litigation procedure, the judge shall record it in accordance with the "Provisions on the Recording, Reporting, and Pursuit of Responsibility for Leading Cadres' Interference in Judicial Activities and Handling of Specific Cases" and the "Provisions on the Recording and Pursuit of Responsibility for Internal Judicial Personnel Inquiring about Cases," as well as their implementation measures.

Article 2: For any requirement from any unit or individual to arrange for a judge to engage in matters beyond the scope of statutory duties, such as investment promotion, administrative law enforcement, public security patrols, traffic direction, sanitation rectification, or public appraisal of work style, the people's court shall refuse, and shall not arrange for judges to engage in the above activities under any pretext.

Personnel of the people's court are strictly prohibited from participating in local investment promotion or joint law enforcement, strictly prohibited from intervening in specific administrative management activities such as land acquisition or house demolition in advance, and must cease participating in temporary bodies such as "Demolition Leading Groups" or "Project Headquarters" led by local authorities.

Article 3: A judge's lawful performance of statutory duties is protected by law, and the judge has the right to independently express opinions on issues such as the admission of evidence, determination of facts, application of law, adjudicative results, and litigation procedures in cases in which he or she participates in adjudication.

Except for participation in professional judge meetings, a judge has the right to refuse to express opinions on cases that have not yet entered the litigation procedure or cases in which he or she did not participate in adjudication.

Article 4: The conduct of a judge in performing statutory duties shall not be subject to investigation of liability for erroneous cases unless after a hearing and deliberation by the Judge Disciplinary Committee. The standards for determining liability for erroneous cases, the scope of investigation, the methods of bearing liability, and the disciplinary procedures shall be separately stipulated by the Supreme People's Court in accordance with the "Several Opinions on Perfecting the Judicial Responsibility System of People's Courts," the "Opinions on Establishing a Disciplinary System for Judges and Prosecutors (Trial)," and relevant work measures.

Without statutory causes and without statutory procedures, a judge shall not be transferred, removed from office, dismissed, or subjected to sanctions such as demotion or removal from post, nor shall the judge's work position be adjusted on grounds such as ranking by case handling quantity, elimination of the last, or unsatisfactory reception of petitions. If a judge is transferred, removed from office, dismissed, or subjected to sanctions such as demotion or removal from post without statutory causes and without statutory procedures, the people's court in which the judge serves shall promptly correct this, or suggest that the relevant authority correct it; if the relevant authority fails to correct it, the court shall report to the superior people's court to request the relevant authority to correct it.

Article 5: The review opinion of the Judge Disciplinary Committee shall be served on the judge concerned and the relevant people's court. A decision to handle a judge by transfer, removal from office, dismissal, or to impose sanctions such as demotion or removal from post shall be made in accordance with statutory procedures. The handling or sanction decision shall be notified in writing to the judge concerned, stating the reasons and basis.

If a judge disagrees with the disciplinary opinion concerning himself or herself, he or she may raise an objection with the Judge Disciplinary Committee that issued the review opinion and apply for a review; if a judge disagrees with the handling or sanction decision concerning himself or herself, he or she may, within 30 days from the date of receipt of the handling or sanction decision, apply for reconsideration to the people's court that made the decision, and has the right to appeal to the superior people's court. A judge shall not be subjected to aggravated punishment for applying for review, reconsideration, or filing an appeal.

The Judge Disciplinary Committee shall review the objection raised by the judge concerned and the reasons therefor, make a decision, and reply in writing to the judge concerned. The people's court that accepts the reconsideration or appeal shall fully hear the judge's statements and explanations; if the original handling or sanction decision is indeed erroneous, it shall be promptly corrected.

Article 6: If an erroneous handling or sanction decision is made against a judge, after the error is corrected, the people's court in which the judge serves shall promptly restore the judge's position, post, rank, and salary benefits, actively restore the judge's reputation, eliminate adverse effects, provide compensation or damages for economic losses as appropriate, and request the relevant authority to investigate the liability of the person who made false accusations or abused power according to law.

If a judge's rank promotion is suspended due to accepting an investigation, but subsequently determined by the relevant authority that no legal or disciplinary liability should be imposed, the time for the rank promotion shall be calculated from the date of suspension.

Article 7: If a state organ or its staff commits any of the following acts, the judge has the right to file a complaint:

(1) Interfering in judicial activities, impeding fair adjudication;

(2) Requiring a judge to engage in matters beyond the scope of statutory duties;

(3) Restricting or suppressing a judge's independent and full expression of opinions on cases in which he or she participated in adjudication;

(4) Exceeding authority or abusing power to transfer, remove from office, dismiss, or impose sanctions such as demotion or removal from post on a judge;

(5) Shirk or delay in handling a judge's requests for protection of lawful performance of duties, or intentionally procrastinating and failing to act;

(6) Neglect of duty or ineffective handling, resulting in infringement of the personal or property rights of a judge or his or her close relatives who are lawfully performing duties;

(7) Infringing on a judge's right to rest or vacation;

(8) Infringing on a judge's right to file complaints or appeals;

(9) Other acts infringing on a judge's statutory rights.

If a people's court or its staff infringes on a judge's statutory rights, and the judge files a complaint with the people's court in which he or she serves or with a superior people's court, the people's court that accepts the complaint shall promptly handle the matter within its authority and notify the judge in writing of the result; if the matter exceeds its authority, it shall promptly transfer the matter to the relevant state organ for handling.

If a state organ other than a people's court or its staff infringes on a judge's statutory rights, the judge may file a complaint with the state power organ, administrative organ, supervisory organ, or prosecutorial organ. The people's court in which the judge serves has the obligation to assist in filing the complaint and providing help, and shall send personnel to report the situation and present opinions to the relevant organ.

Article 8: People's courts at all levels shall improve and perfect the working mechanism of the Judge Evaluation Committee, which shall organize and lead the evaluation and appraisal of judges. The Judge Evaluation Committee shall be composed of the president of the court, relevant court leaders, heads of relevant departments, and several judge representatives. The director shall be the president of the court, and the judge representatives shall be elected by all judges.

The evaluation and appraisal of a judge's trial performance must be conducted by the Judge Evaluation Committee, and the evaluation results shall be made public. If a judge has objections to the evaluation results, he or she may apply for reconsideration.

The evaluation methods and criteria for a judge's trial performance shall set reasonable weight ratios, focus on actual trial work performance, and fully consider differences between regions, trial levels, specialties, departments, and positions, but shall not exceed the judge's statutory duties and professional ethics. The evaluation results and performance appraisals shall serve as important bases for rank promotion, post adjustment, and distribution of performance-based bonuses.

The guiding opinions for the above evaluation shall be uniformly formulated by the Supreme People's Court, and people's courts at all levels shall further refine them in light of local conditions and report them to the superior people's court for recordation.

Article 9: People's courts at all levels shall establish a Judge Rights Protection Committee. The Judge Rights Protection Committee shall be composed of the president of the court, relevant court leaders, heads of relevant departments, and several judge representatives. The director shall be the president of the court, and the judge representatives shall be elected by all judges. The functions of the Judge Rights Protection Committee are:

(1) To centrally receive judges' demands and complaints related to protection of lawful performance of duties;

(2) To organize assessments of risks that a judge or his or her close relatives may face infringement, and take corresponding measures;

(3) To organize assistance to judges whose personal safety, property safety, or residential safety is threatened, or that of their close relatives;

(4) To organize relief for judges whose personal or property rights are infringed, or that of their close relatives;

(5) To assist judges in pursuing the liability of persons who infringe on their statutory rights according to law;

(6) To coordinate arrangements for restoring the reputation of judges who have been subjected to erroneous handling or sanctions, eliminating adverse effects, and providing compensation or damages;

(7) To guide judges in correctly and effectively protecting their own lawful rights and interests, and organize relevant training and psychological counseling;

(8) To supervise inspections of the construction of safety inspection facilities, protective isolation systems, safety equipment, and safety protection mechanisms of the court;

(9) To coordinate and guide the court's judicial police department and organ security department in maintaining courtroom order, organ security, judge personal protection, and various emergency response measures;

(10) To establish early warning, emergency, and coordination mechanisms with public security organs, news authorities, and network regulatory departments related to the protection of judges' lawful performance of duties;

(11) Other matters related to the protection of judges and trial support personnel in lawfully performing their duties.

The specific work of the Judge Rights Protection Committee of a people's court at any level shall be undertaken by the court's personnel management department.

The Judge Rights Protection Committee of a superior people's court shall supervise and guide the work of the Judge Rights Protection Committee of people's courts within its jurisdiction. If the Judge Rights Protection Committee of a people's court at the same level fails to effectively protect a judge's lawful performance of duties, the judge may file a complaint with the Judge Rights Protection Committee of the superior people's court.

Article 10: The areas for case filing, petition reception, litigation services, and trials of people's courts at all levels shall be relatively separated from judges' office areas, and equipped with one-key alarm devices to facilitate timely handling of emergencies.

People's courts at all levels shall strictly implement the "Standards for Security Personnel and Equipment Configuration of People's Courts" and the "Standards for Police Equipment Configuration for Different Duty Posts of Judicial Police of People's Courts," generally establish security checkpoints, equip corresponding safety equipment, and strengthen the sense of responsibility, regulatory awareness, and operational skills of security personnel.

People's courts shall equip judges and trial support personnel with office telephones capable of recording and recording devices capable of video recording to facilitate timely recording and storage of information of an interfering, inquiring, threatening, or insulting nature.

People's courts shall provide judges and trial support personnel with special meeting and reception areas equipped with audio and video recording facilities. When meeting or receiving parties or their agents outside the trial courtroom, a judge may request to do so in a special area and has the right to refuse a party's or agent's request for a unilateral meeting or reception.

Article 11: People's courts at all levels shall maintain courtroom order according to law. For persons who violate courtroom rules and disrupt courtroom order, depending on the severity of the circumstances, measures such as verbal warnings and restraint, reprimand, ordering to sign a statement of repentance, ordering to leave the courtroom, forcible removal from the courtroom, fines, or detention shall be taken according to law; for persons who seriously disrupt courtroom order and commit crimes such as the crime of disrupting courtroom order, criminal liability shall be pursued according to law.

For persons who commit any of the following acts in other areas of the people's court outside the trial courtroom, measures such as reprimand, restraint, control, or removal from the scene shall be promptly taken, relevant evidence shall be collected and preserved, and the matter shall be promptly transferred to the public security organ for handling; if the act constitutes crimes such as the crime of illegally carrying guns, ammunition, controlled instruments, or dangerous substances endangering public safety, the crime of obstructing public affairs, the crime of picking quarrels and provoking trouble, or the crime of intentionally destroying property, criminal liability shall be pursued according to law:

(1) Illegally carrying controlled instruments or dangerous substances, evading or refusing security checks;

(2) Without permission, forcibly entering the judge's office area or trial area;

(3) Loud noise, disturbance, refusing to heed persuasion, seriously disrupting office order;

(4) Insulting, slandering, threatening, or assaulting personnel of the people's court or litigation participants;

(5) Damaging court buildings, office facilities, or vehicles;

(6) Seizing or damaging litigation documents or evidence;

(7) Lingering after working hours, refusing to heed persuasion, refusing to leave;

(8) Intentionally abandoning persons who cannot take care of themselves due to old age, young age, frailty, severe illness, or physical disability;

(9) Threatening personnel of the people's court by suicide or self-harm;

(10) Other acts endangering the security of the people's court or disrupting office order.

For persons who sit in protests, distribute materials, shout slogans, or hang banners around the people's court, the people's court shall request the public security organ to handle the matter according to law; if the act endangers the personal safety of personnel of the people's court, the organ security department may, together with judicial police, carry out relevant emergency response measures and promptly request the public security organ to handle the matter according to law; if the act constitutes crimes such as the crime of gathering a crowd to assault a state organ, the crime of gathering a crowd to disturb social order, the crime of gathering a crowd to disturb traffic order, the crime of gathering a crowd to disturb public place order, or the crime of obstructing public affairs, criminal liability shall be pursued according to law.

Article 12: For persons who disclose or disseminate information that should not be publicly disclosed concerning a judge or his or her close relatives, or who spy, secretly photograph, eavesdrop, or spread the privacy of a judge or his or her close relatives, the people's court shall request the public security organ to handle the matter according to law; if the act constitutes crimes such as the crime of infringing on citizens' personal information, criminal liability shall be pursued according to law.

People's courts shall fully utilize platforms such as the litigation service center, the 12368 litigation service hotline, and the litigation service website for querying information, answering inquiries, and contacting judges, so as to avoid excessive disclosure of information affecting the judge's trial work and daily life. When disclosing information beyond the judge's name, photo, position, rank, office telephone, and work email through the trial procedure information disclosure platform, the judge's consent shall be obtained.

Article 13: If a judge's reputation is harmed due to false reports, false accusations, or defamation using information networks or other means as a result of lawful performance of duties, the people's court in which the judge serves shall promptly clarify the facts through official websites, Weibo, WeChat public accounts, or press conferences to eliminate adverse effects, maintain the judge's good reputation, and jointly pursue the liability of the relevant unit or individual with the relevant authorities according to law.

Article 14: People's courts shall strictly punish, according to law, illegal or criminal acts such as interfering with or obstructing judicial activities, intimidating, threatening, retaliating, making false accusations, slandering, or violently assaulting judges or their close relatives.

If a judge, due to lawful performance of statutory duties, or his or her close relatives, encounters intimidation, threats, harassment, stalking, or physical, property, or residential infringement or damage, the people's court in which the judge serves shall promptly take protective measures and request the public security organ to handle the matter according to law; if the act constitutes crimes such as the crime of intentional homicide, the crime of intentional injury, the crime of picking quarrels and provoking trouble, the crime of intentionally destroying property, or the crime of illegal entry into a residence, criminal liability shall be pursued according to law; if the perpetrator is a mental patient, compulsory medical treatment shall be ordered according to law.

Article 15: When a people's court tries cases with high danger, such as crimes of terrorist activities, crimes of organized crime of a triad nature, major drug crimes, or crimes of cult organizations, it shall take necessary protective measures for judges and their close relatives, such as trial protection, prohibiting contact with specific persons, and other necessary measures. For close relatives of judges, measures to conceal their identity may also be taken. When handling other cases with high danger, upon the judge's application, the above protective measures shall be taken for the judge and his or her close relatives.

Article 16: People's courts at all levels shall, in coordination with relevant departments, pay judges' basic wages, allowances, and subsidies in full and on time. The distribution of performance-based bonuses shall follow the orientation of trial performance, adhere to the principles of openness, fairness, and impartiality, shall not be linked to the judge's rank or administrative grade, and shall focus on tilting toward front-line personnel.

Article 17: People's courts at all levels shall provide psychological counseling and relief services for judges, generally establish and earnestly implement the annual physical examination system for judges, ensure that each judge receives a comprehensive physical examination once a year, and coordinate with relevant departments to improve the medical security system and preferential treatment measures for judges, providing protection for judges' personal, property, and medical rights commensurate with their occupational risks.

Article 18: People's courts at all levels shall, centered on trial work needs, comprehensively adopt methods such as centralized off-the-job training, online video teaching, and circuit teaching to ensure that all judges regularly participate in various professional training, focusing on improving their ability to preside over trials, apply the law, draft judgment documents, and use information technology. Each judge shall participate in at least one off-the-job professional training per year.

Article 19: People's courts at all levels shall protect judges' right to rest and vacation according to law, earnestly implement systems such as annual vacation, effectively guarantee judges' necessary vacation time, and include the implementation of judges' vacation in the performance evaluation scope of each department, and shall not in any way obstruct judges' vacation.

People's courts at all levels shall, based on the laws of adjudication and actual court conditions, reasonably calculate the saturation of judges' work, scientifically determine the workload of judges, and timely adjust the staffing of judge positions or supplement trial support personnel, and shall not force judges to work overtime beyond statutory working days.

Article 20: When a superior people's court selects judges from a lower people's court, it shall, in coordination with relevant departments, improve and perfect supporting safeguard measures to ensure that judges selected from other localities can perform their duties with peace of mind.

Article 21: The protection of trial support personnel in lawfully performing their statutory duties shall be governed by these Measures mutatis mutandis.

Article 22: For the protection of military court judges in lawfully performing their statutory duties, if military regulations have provisions, those provisions shall prevail.

Article 23: The interpretation of these Measures shall be the responsibility of the Supreme People's Court.

Article 24: These Measures shall come into force on the date of issuance.
